package com.shahaabapps.core.content

/**
 * AZ-305 mock exam: design scenarios across every module, timed like the real
 * sitting, with no feedback until the end. Pass at 70 percent, the 700 of
 * 1000 the real exam asks for.
 */
internal object Az305Mock {
    val quiz: Quiz = Quiz(
        id = "sa-mock", title = "AZ-305 mock exam", passPercent = 70, timeLimitMinutes = 100, instantFeedback = false, drawCount = 50,
        questions = B.spread(listOf(
            // Identity, governance and monitoring: logging and monitoring
            B.q("sa-mk1", "sa-monitoring", "You need one place where the operations team can query logs from all three subscriptions with one query language and keep them for 18 months. What do you recommend?", listOf("A single Log Analytics workspace, with diagnostic settings on each resource sending logs to it", "A storage account in each subscription, with diagnostic settings archiving the logs as blobs", "An event hub namespace that every resource streams its logs to through diagnostic settings", "The activity log of each subscription, with its retention raised to 18 months in the portal"), setOf(0), "A Log Analytics workspace collects logs from many subscriptions into one store you can search with KQL (Kusto Query Language), and its retention can be set well beyond 18 months. Diagnostic settings are how each resource sends its logs there.",
                scenario = "Wrenfield Clinics runs 40 web apps and databases spread over three Azure subscriptions. When something breaks, the operations team wants to search every log in one place with one query, and auditors ask that logs stay searchable for 18 months.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Blobs in storage are cheap to keep, but you cannot search across them with one query like a workspace allows.",
                    2 to "An event hub is a pipe that streams logs onwards and keeps them for 90 days at most. It is not a store you can query.",
                    3 to "The activity log only records control actions such as creating or deleting resources, and Azure keeps it for 90 days. You cannot raise that; you can only export it elsewhere.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk2", "sa-monitoring", "The security team's existing third-party SIEM must receive Azure resource logs within seconds. Where should the diagnostic settings send the logs?", listOf("To a Log Analytics workspace", "To an Azure event hub that the SIEM reads from", "To a storage account that the SIEM polls once a day", "To Application Insights"), setOf(1), "Diagnostic settings can stream logs to Azure Event Hubs. An event hub is a high-speed pipe that outside tools, such as a third-party SIEM (security information and event management tool), read in near real time.",
                scenario = "Saltmarsh Shipping keeps its security monitoring in a third-party SIEM product that runs in its own data centre. The security team will not move to a new tool, but it wants Azure logs to arrive in the SIEM within seconds.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    0 to "A workspace stores logs for Azure Monitor queries. It does not feed a third-party tool.",
                    2 to "Daily polling fails the within-seconds requirement.",
                    3 to "Application Insights watches application performance. It is not a route out to other tools.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk3", "sa-monitoring", "Which service should you recommend to track response times, failed requests and calls to the database from the web app, with a map of how the parts call each other?", listOf("Application Insights", "Azure Service Health", "Network Watcher", "Azure Advisor"), setOf(0), "Application Insights is the part of Azure Monitor that watches a running application: request rates, response times, failures, dependency calls such as database queries, and an application map of how components talk to each other.",
                scenario = "Kestrel Games has a web API that players say is slow in the evenings. The developers want to see which requests are slow, which of them fail, and whether the database calls behind them are the cause.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Service Health tells you about problems on Azure's side, not inside your own code.",
                    2 to "Network Watcher diagnoses network paths and traffic, not application requests.",
                    3 to "Advisor gives general best-practice recommendations. It does not trace live requests.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk4", "sa-monitoring", "You must collect Windows event logs and performance counters from the Azure VMs and from the on-premises servers, with one configuration. What do you recommend? Select two.", listOf("Install the Azure Monitor Agent and define what to collect in data collection rules", "Connect the on-premises servers to Azure with Azure Arc", "Install the legacy Log Analytics agent on every server", "Enable boot diagnostics on each VM"), setOf(0, 1), "The Azure Monitor Agent collects guest operating system data, and data collection rules say what to collect and where to send it. Azure Arc makes on-premises servers appear in Azure, so the same agent and rules apply to them too.",
                scenario = "Driftmoor Logistics has 60 Windows VMs in Azure and 25 Windows servers still in its own warehouse offices. The operations lead wants one collection setup for all of them, sending event logs and performance counters to one workspace.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    2 to "The legacy Log Analytics agent has been retired. The Azure Monitor Agent replaces it.",
                    3 to "Boot diagnostics captures a screenshot and serial log at start-up, not ongoing event logs.",
                ),
            ),
            // Identity
            B.q("sa-mk5", "sa-identity", "Users must sign in to Microsoft 365 and Azure with their existing on-premises passwords, and sign-in must keep working if the on-premises data centre goes offline. Which sign-in method do you recommend?", listOf("Password hash synchronization", "Pass-through authentication", "Federation with AD FS", "Cloud-only accounts with new passwords"), setOf(0), "Password hash synchronization copies a hash (a scrambled, one-way form) of each password to Microsoft Entra ID, so Entra ID checks sign-ins itself. Nothing on-premises has to be running at sign-in time, and it needs the least extra infrastructure.",
                scenario = "Quillmark Publishing has 800 staff in an on-premises Active Directory. It wants the simplest setup with the fewest extra servers, and it worries about losing cloud sign-in when its single data centre has a power cut.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Pass-through authentication sends each sign-in to agents on-premises, so an outage there stops cloud sign-in.",
                    2 to "AD FS needs its own server farm on-premises, and sign-in fails when that farm is unreachable.",
                    3 to "New cloud-only passwords break the requirement to keep the existing passwords.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk6", "sa-identity", "Customers must be able to sign up to the new booking app with an email address or a social account, kept apart from the employee directory. What do you recommend?", listOf("Microsoft Entra External ID in an external tenant", "Invite each customer as a B2B guest into the workforce tenant", "Create a new Azure AD B2C tenant", "Create customer accounts in the on-premises Active Directory and synchronize them"), setOf(0), "Microsoft Entra External ID with an external tenant is made for customer identity: self-service sign-up, social and email accounts, and a directory kept separate from staff accounts.",
                scenario = "Pebblecrest Hotels is building a booking app for the public. Guests should create their own account with an email address or a Google account. The security team insists that guest accounts never sit in the same directory as staff.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "B2B guests are for business partners in your workforce tenant, which mixes customers with staff.",
                    2 to "Azure AD B2C stopped being sold to new customers in May 2025; External ID is the path for new apps.",
                    3 to "Putting the public into the company Active Directory is the opposite of keeping them apart.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk7", "sa-identity", "Engineers from a partner company must use an internal web app with their own company accounts, and must lose access when the partner removes them. What do you recommend?", listOf("Microsoft Entra B2B collaboration, inviting them as guest users", "Create a member account for each engineer in your tenant", "Share one service account for the whole partner team", "Federate the app directly with the partner's AD FS server"), setOf(0), "B2B collaboration adds the partner's people as guests who sign in with their own home accounts. When their home organisation disables an account, that person can no longer sign in to your app.",
                scenario = "Tidewell Energy works with an engineering partner whose 30 engineers need the internal maintenance-planning app. Tidewell does not want to manage passwords for them, and leavers at the partner must lose access at once.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Member accounts mean Tidewell manages new passwords, and leavers keep access until someone remembers to remove them.",
                    2 to "A shared account cannot tell people apart and breaks every audit rule.",
                    3 to "Per-app federation is far more work than B2B and has to be built again for every app.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk8", "sa-identity", "Several Azure Functions apps and a VM must all use the same identity to reach a storage account, and that identity must survive when any one of them is deleted. What do you recommend?", listOf("A user-assigned managed identity attached to each of them", "A system-assigned managed identity on each of them", "A service principal with a client secret stored in app settings", "A shared storage account key"), setOf(0), "A user-assigned managed identity is a standalone Azure resource that you attach to many services. It keeps living when one of those services is deleted, and Azure handles its credentials, so no secret is stored.",
                scenario = "Orchardine Foods has four Functions apps and one VM that all write to the same storage account. The team wants to grant access once, not five times, and avoid any stored secret.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A system-assigned identity belongs to one resource and is deleted with it, so access would be granted five times.",
                    2 to "A client secret is exactly the stored credential the team wants to avoid.",
                    3 to "A storage key gives full access to the whole account and is a secret that must be rotated.",
                ),
            ),
            // Authorization and secrets
            B.q("sa-mk9", "sa-authorization", "The support team must be able to start, stop and restart VMs in one resource group, and nothing else. What is the best approach?", listOf("Create a custom role with only the VM start, restart and deallocate actions, and assign it at the resource group scope", "Assign the built-in Virtual Machine Contributor role to the support team at the resource group scope", "Create a custom role with only the VM start, restart and deallocate actions, and assign it at the subscription scope", "Assign the built-in Virtual Machine User Login role to the support team at the resource group scope"), setOf(0), "When no built-in role matches the job, a custom Azure role lists just the actions needed. Assigning it at the resource group keeps the reach as small as the requirement.",
                scenario = "Harrowgate Bank's night support team restarts stuck VMs in the payments resource group. The auditors require least privilege: the team may power the VMs on and off, but must not create, delete or resize anything.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Virtual Machine Contributor can also create, delete and resize VMs, which the auditors forbid.",
                    2 to "The actions are right, but the subscription scope reaches the VMs in every resource group, not just payments.",
                    3 to "Virtual Machine User Login lets people view VMs and sign in to them. It cannot start, restart or stop them.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk10", "sa-authorization", "A reporting app must read blobs in one container using its managed identity. Which role do you assign, and where?", listOf("Storage Blob Data Reader on that container", "Reader on the storage account", "Storage Account Contributor on the storage account", "Storage Blob Data Owner on the subscription"), setOf(0), "Reading blob contents is a data plane action, so it needs a data role such as Storage Blob Data Reader. Scoping it to the one container gives no more than the app needs.",
                scenario = "Corvelle Insurance has a nightly reporting app that reads claim files from one blob container. Other containers in the same account hold medical records the app must never see.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Reader lets you see the storage account's settings, but not read the blobs inside it.",
                    2 to "Storage Account Contributor manages the account and can reach its keys, which opens every container.",
                    3 to "Owner over the whole subscription can read and change every blob everywhere.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk11", "sa-authorization", "Keys must live in a single-tenant hardware security module that the company alone controls, managed as an Azure service. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Key Vault Managed HSM", "Azure Key Vault Standard tier", "Azure Key Vault Premium tier", "Store the keys as app settings in App Service"), setOf(0), "Managed HSM gives you a pool of hardware security modules (tamper-resistant key hardware) used by your organisation alone, and you hold its security domain, so Microsoft cannot use the keys. Premium uses HSMs too, but shared between customers.",
                scenario = "Harrowgate Bank's regulator requires that encryption keys for customer data live in dedicated, single-tenant HSM hardware whose security domain the bank alone controls. The bank does not want to run its own hardware.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Standard protects keys in software, not in hardware security modules.",
                    2 to "Premium uses HSMs, but they are multi-tenant, shared with other customers.",
                    3 to "App settings are plain configuration, not a key store at all.",
                ),
            ),
            // Governance
            B.q("sa-mk12", "sa-governance", "Each business unit has several subscriptions, and policies must apply to all of a unit's subscriptions, including new ones, with the least admin effort. What structure do you recommend?", listOf("A management group per business unit, with the subscriptions under it and policies assigned at the management group", "One resource group per business unit inside a single subscription, with policies assigned at each resource group", "A management group per environment (production, test, development), with policies assigned at each management group", "One policy initiative per business unit, assigned separately on each subscription that the unit owns"), setOf(0), "A management group is a folder above subscriptions. Policies and role assignments made on it flow down to every subscription inside, including subscriptions added later.",
                scenario = "Brackenridge Tools has three business units, each with production, test and development subscriptions. Every new subscription a unit creates must get that unit's policies automatically.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Cramming units into one subscription hits limits and mixes billing and access boundaries.",
                    2 to "Each environment group would hold subscriptions from all three units, so one unit's policies would land on the other units too.",
                    3 to "An initiative bundles policies, but assigning it subscription by subscription is the effort ruled out, and new subscriptions are easily missed.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk13", "sa-governance", "Every resource must carry the CostCentre tag of its resource group, and resources created without it must get it automatically. What do you recommend?", listOf("Assign the built-in Azure Policy that inherits a tag from the resource group, with the modify effect", "Nothing extra: resources created in a resource group take on that group's tags by default", "Assign the built-in Azure Policy that requires a tag on resources, with the deny effect", "Assign a custom Azure RBAC role that only lets developers create resources that carry tags"), setOf(0), "Tags are not inherited by default. Azure Policy has built-in definitions that copy a tag from the resource group onto resources, using the modify effect, and a remediation task can fix resources that already exist.",
                scenario = "Copperleaf Foods' finance team splits the Azure bill by the CostCentre tag. Each resource group already carries the tag, but developers keep creating resources without it.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Resources do not inherit tags from their resource group unless something like a policy copies them.",
                    2 to "The deny effect blocks untagged resources instead of adding the tag for them, so nothing is filled in automatically.",
                    3 to "An RBAC role lists the actions people may take. It cannot check tags on a new resource or add a missing one.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk14", "sa-governance", "Administrators must not hold the Global Administrator role all the time. They should activate it for a few hours, with approval and a reason. What do you recommend?", listOf("Microsoft Entra Privileged Identity Management", "Microsoft Entra Conditional Access policies", "Microsoft Entra ID Governance access reviews", "Microsoft Entra ID Protection for risky sign-ins"), setOf(0), "Privileged Identity Management (PIM) makes people eligible for a role rather than permanent holders. They activate it for a limited time, and you can require approval, a reason and multifactor sign-in.",
                scenario = "After an audit, Larchmere Media decided that no one should be a standing Global Administrator. Admins should switch the role on only when needed, and a second person must approve each time.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Conditional Access controls how people sign in. It does not make roles temporary.",
                    2 to "Access reviews check who still needs access every so often, but the role stays permanent between reviews.",
                    3 to "ID Protection detects risky users and sign-ins. It does not make roles temporary or ask anyone to approve them.",
                ),
            ),
            // Data: relational
            B.q("sa-mk15", "sa-relational", "The SQL Server databases use SQL Server Agent jobs, cross-database queries and CLR code. They must move to a managed platform service with as few code changes as possible. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure SQL Managed Instance", "Azure SQL Database single databases", "Azure Cosmos DB for NoSQL", "Azure Database for PostgreSQL flexible server"), setOf(0), "SQL Managed Instance is a PaaS (platform as a service) form of SQL Server with near-complete compatibility, including SQL Server Agent, cross-database queries and CLR, so the databases move with few changes and no servers to patch.",
                scenario = "Sunvale Schools runs its student records system on SQL Server 2016 in a cupboard server room. The app relies on Agent jobs, queries that join across three databases and some CLR functions. The IT team wants no more servers to patch.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Single databases do not support SQL Server Agent or cross-database queries the way an instance does.",
                    2 to "Cosmos DB is a NoSQL database, which would mean rewriting the app.",
                    3 to "PostgreSQL is a different database engine, which also means rewriting the SQL.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk16", "sa-relational", "A 40 TB database keeps growing, and the team wants fast backups and restores regardless of size, plus extra read-only replicas. Which Azure SQL Database service tier fits?", listOf("Hyperscale", "General Purpose", "Business Critical", "Basic"), setOf(0), "Hyperscale separates storage from compute so a database can grow up to 128 TB, backups are near-instant snapshots, restores take minutes rather than hours, and you can add read-only replicas.",
                scenario = "Glimmerton Games stores match history in Azure SQL Database. It is 40 TB and grows by 2 TB a month. Restoring a copy for testing currently takes most of a day.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "General Purpose has a much lower size limit and restores slow down as the database grows.",
                    2 to "Business Critical also has a much lower size limit than Hyperscale.",
                    3 to "Basic is for small, light databases of a few gigabytes.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk17", "sa-relational", "Each customer has its own small database, and their busy times are unpredictable and rarely overlap. How do you keep cost down without hurting performance?", listOf("Put the databases in an elastic pool", "Give each database its own provisioned compute sized for its peak", "Merge all customers into one database on the Business Critical tier", "Move the databases to SQL Server on a single VM"), setOf(0), "An elastic pool shares one set of compute between many databases. Because their peaks rarely coincide, the pool can be much smaller than the sum of every database's peak.",
                scenario = "Ravenmoor Software sells a SaaS (software as a service) booking product with 300 customers, each with its own database. Most databases sit idle, then spike for an hour at different times of day.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Sizing each one for its peak pays for 300 peaks that almost never happen together.",
                    2 to "Merging customers is a big redesign, and Business Critical is the most expensive tier.",
                    3 to "A VM brings back patching and management and is a single point of failure.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk18", "sa-relational", "Card numbers must stay encrypted even from database administrators, and only the application may see them in clear text. What do you recommend?", listOf("Always Encrypted", "Transparent data encryption", "Dynamic data masking", "TLS for connections"), setOf(0), "Always Encrypted encrypts chosen columns inside the client driver, and the keys stay with the application. The database, and anyone administering it, only ever sees the scrambled values.",
                scenario = "Quayside Retail stores loyalty card numbers in Azure SQL Database. The compliance team says that even the database administrators must never be able to read them.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "TDE encrypts the files on disk, but the database decrypts data for anyone allowed to query it, admins included.",
                    2 to "Masking only hides values in query results for some users. Admins can see them or turn it off.",
                    3 to "TLS protects data while it travels, not from people who can query the database.",
                ),
            ),
            // Data: semi-structured and unstructured
            B.q("sa-mk19", "sa-nonrelational", "Players in Europe, Asia and America must save progress with single-digit millisecond latency, writing to the nearest region. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Cosmos DB with several regions and multi-region writes enabled", "Azure Cosmos DB with several regions and a single write region", "Azure SQL Database with active geo-replication to several regions", "Azure Table storage with read-access geo-zone-redundant storage"), setOf(0), "Cosmos DB is built for global apps: you add regions, and with multi-region writes every region accepts writes locally, with single-digit millisecond latency backed by its SLA.",
                scenario = "Glimmerton Games is launching a mobile game worldwide. Each save is a small JSON document. The game must feel instant everywhere, so each player must write to a nearby region.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Extra regions serve local reads, but with one write region every save from a far-away player crosses the world.",
                    2 to "Geo-replicas in SQL Database are read-only; all writes still go to one primary region.",
                    3 to "Read-access geo-zone-redundant storage (RA-GZRS) adds a readable copy in a second region, but all writes still go to the one primary region.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk20", "sa-nonrelational", "Forms are opened often for 30 days, rarely for a year, and then must be kept for seven years but almost never read. How do you keep storage cost lowest with the least effort?", listOf("A lifecycle management policy that moves blobs to cool after 30 days and to archive after a year", "Keep everything in the hot tier", "Put every form straight into the archive tier", "A Functions app that copies old forms to a second storage account each night"), setOf(0), "Lifecycle management rules move blobs between access tiers automatically by age. Cool costs less to store than hot, and archive costs least of all, with slower and dearer reads that suit data almost never read.",
                scenario = "Thistledown Health stores scanned forms as blobs. Staff open new forms often for a month, then rarely, and the law says they must be kept for seven years.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Hot storage is the most expensive tier to keep data in for years.",
                    2 to "Archive blobs take hours to rehydrate, which fails the busy first month.",
                    3 to "Custom code is effort to build and run, and a second account does not change the price tier.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk21", "sa-nonrelational", "Audit files must be impossible to change or delete for seven years, even by an account owner. What do you configure?", listOf("Immutable storage with a locked time-based retention policy on the container", "Immutable storage with an unlocked time-based retention policy on the container", "Immutable storage with a legal hold on the container, to be cleared after seven years", "A CanNotDelete lock on the storage account, with blob soft delete turned on"), setOf(0), "Immutable storage keeps blobs in a WORM (write once, read many) state. Once a time-based retention policy is locked, nobody, including an administrator, can change or delete the blobs until the period ends.",
                scenario = "Harrowgate Bank's regulator demands that trading records are stored in a write-once form for seven years, protected even from a rogue administrator.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "An unlocked policy can be shortened or deleted, so a rogue administrator could remove the protection.",
                    2 to "A legal hold lasts until someone with the right permission clears it, so it does not guarantee seven years.",
                    3 to "A lock protects the storage account resource, not the blobs inside, and an admin can remove it. Soft delete keeps data for at most 365 days.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk22", "sa-nonrelational", "Branch offices need fast local access to the same file shares, while Azure holds the full copy. What do you recommend? Select two.", listOf("Azure Files as the cloud copy of the shares", "Azure File Sync with a Windows Server cache in each branch", "Azure Blob Storage with the archive tier", "Azure Data Box shipped to each branch"), setOf(0, 1), "Azure Files holds the shares in the cloud. Azure File Sync turns a Windows Server in each branch into a fast local cache of those shares and keeps every copy in step.",
                scenario = "Driftmoor Logistics has eight branch offices with slow internet links. Staff open large shipping documents all day and complain when files load from far away.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    2 to "Archive is offline storage that takes hours to read, the opposite of fast access.",
                    3 to "Data Box is a one-off shipping device for moving data, not an ongoing cache.",
                ),
            ),
            // Data integration
            B.q("sa-mk23", "sa-data-integration", "Every night, data must be copied from an on-premises SQL Server into Azure Data Lake Storage, with the job scheduled and monitored in Azure. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Data Factory with a self-hosted integration runtime installed on-premises", "Azure Data Factory with only the Azure integration runtime", "Azure Stream Analytics", "Azure Event Grid"), setOf(0), "Data Factory orchestrates copy and transform jobs on a schedule. To reach a data source inside a private network, you install a self-hosted integration runtime there; it acts as Data Factory's hands on-premises.",
                scenario = "Copperleaf Foods' sales figures live in a SQL Server in its head office, behind the firewall. Analysts want last night's data in the data lake by 6 a.m. each day.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "The Azure integration runtime runs in Azure and cannot reach a server inside a private office network.",
                    2 to "Stream Analytics processes live event streams, not nightly batch copies from a database.",
                    3 to "Event Grid delivers small event notifications. It does not copy data.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk24", "sa-data-integration", "Sensor readings arrive as a stream, and an alert must fire within seconds when a five-minute average passes a limit. Which service processes the stream?", listOf("Azure Stream Analytics", "Azure Data Factory", "Azure Synapse Analytics dedicated SQL pool", "Microsoft Purview"), setOf(0), "Stream Analytics runs SQL-like queries over data while it is still flowing, including time windows such as a rolling five-minute average, and can send results to an alert path in seconds.",
                scenario = "Tidewell Energy's wind turbines send vibration readings to Azure Event Hubs every second. Engineers want a warning when any turbine's five-minute average vibration goes too high.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Data Factory runs batch jobs on a schedule, so it is too slow for an alert within seconds.",
                    2 to "A dedicated SQL pool is a data warehouse for stored data, not a stream processor.",
                    3 to "Purview catalogues and governs data. It does not process streams.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk25", "sa-data-integration", "The company needs to know what data it holds across Azure and on-premises, where sensitive data sits and where each report's data came from. What do you recommend?", listOf("Microsoft Purview", "Azure Monitor", "Azure Data Factory", "Azure Policy"), setOf(0), "Microsoft Purview scans data sources to build a catalogue (what data exists), classifies sensitive data, and shows lineage: the path data took from source to report.",
                scenario = "After a GDPR request took three weeks to answer, Pebblecrest Hotels wants a searchable map of all its data stores, flags on personal data, and a trail showing where each report gets its numbers.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Azure Monitor collects logs and metrics about systems, not a catalogue of business data.",
                    2 to "Data Factory moves data. It can feed lineage to Purview but is not a catalogue itself.",
                    3 to "Azure Policy governs resource settings, not what data is inside them.",
                ),
            ),
            // Business continuity: backup and DR
            B.q("sa-mk26", "sa-backup-dr", "If the main region fails, the VMs must run in a second region within an hour, losing at most a few minutes of data, with orchestrated failover. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Site Recovery replicating the VMs to the second region", "Azure Backup with a daily backup policy", "Availability zones in the main region", "Copying managed disk snapshots to the second region each night"), setOf(0), "Site Recovery continuously replicates VMs to another region, so the recovery point is minutes old. Recovery plans fail over a group of VMs in order, which meets a short recovery time objective (RTO).",
                scenario = "Corvelle Insurance's claims system runs on 12 VMs in one region. The business requires an RTO of one hour and an RPO (recovery point objective, the data you can afford to lose) of 15 minutes if the whole region goes down.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A daily backup can lose up to a day of data, and restoring 12 VMs by hand is slow.",
                    2 to "Zones protect against a data centre failure, not the loss of a whole region.",
                    3 to "Nightly snapshots lose up to a day of data, and rebuilding the VMs is manual.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk27", "sa-backup-dr", "Backups must survive an attacker who has stolen an administrator's account. What do you configure on the Recovery Services vault? Select two.", listOf("Make the vault immutable and lock the setting", "Turn on multi-user authorization with a Resource Guard owned by another team", "Reduce backup retention to seven days", "Store the backups in the same resource group as the VMs"), setOf(0, 1), "An immutable vault blocks any action that would delete recovery points early. Multi-user authorization (MUA) makes critical operations need approval through a Resource Guard owned by a different team, so one stolen account is not enough.",
                scenario = "Sunvale Schools heard of a school whose backups were deleted by ransomware attackers using an IT admin's account. It wants its VM backups to survive even if one admin account is compromised.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    2 to "Shorter retention gives attackers less to delete, not less ability to delete it.",
                    3 to "Where the backups sit does not stop someone with admin rights from deleting them.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk28", "sa-backup-dr", "Monthly backups of an Azure SQL database must be kept for ten years. What do you recommend?", listOf("Configure a long-term retention (LTR) policy on the database", "Raise point-in-time restore retention to ten years", "Export a BACPAC file by hand each month", "Use active geo-replication"), setOf(0), "Point-in-time restore keeps backups for at most 35 days. Long-term retention keeps chosen weekly, monthly or yearly full backups for up to ten years, automatically.",
                scenario = "Harrowgate Bank's auditors require a restorable copy of the loans database for every month of the past ten years.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Point-in-time restore cannot go past 35 days.",
                    2 to "Manual exports are easy to forget and are not a managed backup.",
                    3 to "A geo-replica is a live copy; if data is deleted on the primary it is deleted on the replica too.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk29", "sa-backup-dr", "Users sometimes overwrite or delete blobs by mistake, and you must be able to bring back an earlier version for 30 days. What do you enable? Select two.", listOf("Blob versioning", "Blob soft delete with 30-day retention", "The archive access tier", "Geo-redundant storage (GRS)"), setOf(0, 1), "Versioning keeps the previous state of a blob every time it is overwritten. Soft delete keeps deleted blobs recoverable for the period you set. Together they undo both kinds of accident.",
                scenario = "Larchmere Media's editors upload video edits to blob storage. Twice this month someone overwrote a finished cut with an old one, and once a whole folder was deleted.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    2 to "Archive lowers storage cost. It does not keep old versions.",
                    3 to "GRS copies every change, mistakes included, to the second region.",
                ),
            ),
            // High availability
            B.q("sa-mk30", "sa-high-availability", "The web tier must keep running if one data centre in the region fails, with the highest VM uptime SLA. How do you place the VMs?", listOf("Spread two or more VMs across availability zones", "Put two or more VMs in an availability set", "Run one VM with premium SSD disks", "Put all VMs in a proximity placement group"), setOf(0), "Availability zones are separate data centres in one region, with their own power and cooling. VMs spread across two or more zones survive the loss of a whole data centre and get a 99.99 percent SLA.",
                scenario = "Pebblecrest Hotels' booking site runs on VMs in one Azure region that has availability zones. The board wants the site to survive the loss of a whole data centre.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "An availability set protects against a rack or update failure inside one data centre, not the loss of the data centre.",
                    2 to "A single VM, however fast its disks, is a single point of failure.",
                    3 to "A proximity group keeps VMs close together, which makes them more likely to fail together.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk31", "sa-high-availability", "The Azure SQL database must keep running if a data centre in the region fails, without any app changes. What do you configure?", listOf("Zone redundancy on the database", "A long-term retention policy", "A second database in another region, kept in step by a nightly job", "Read scale-out"), setOf(0), "Zone-redundant configuration places the database's replicas in different availability zones in the same region. If one zone fails, the database keeps serving from another, and the connection string does not change.",
                scenario = "Ravenmoor Software's main database runs in a region with availability zones. The uptime promise to customers requires surviving a data centre failure, and the developers do not want to change connection strings.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "LTR keeps old backups. It does nothing to keep the database online.",
                    2 to "A nightly copy loses up to a day of data and needs a manual switch.",
                    3 to "Read scale-out offloads read queries. It does not protect against a zone failure.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk32", "sa-high-availability", "Data must survive a data centre failure and a full region outage, and the app must be able to read from the second region during an outage. Which redundancy option fits?", listOf("Read-access geo-zone-redundant storage (RA-GZRS)", "Geo-zone-redundant storage (GZRS) in two regions", "Zone-redundant storage (ZRS) across three zones", "Geo-redundant storage (GRS) in two regions"), setOf(0), "GZRS keeps three copies across zones in the main region plus copies in a paired second region. The RA (read-access) form lets the app read from the second region at any time, even during an outage.",
                scenario = "Thistledown Health stores patient letters in blob storage. The letters must survive any single data centre or region failure, and the patient portal must still show them while the main region is down.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "GZRS survives both kinds of failure, but you cannot read from the second region until a failover happens.",
                    2 to "ZRS survives a data centre failure, but not a whole region outage.",
                    3 to "GRS copies to a second region, but you cannot read from there until a failover happens.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk33", "sa-high-availability", "Two VMs run an app in a region that has no availability zones. How do you protect it from hardware failures and planned maintenance?", listOf("Put both VMs in an availability set", "Put both VMs on one Azure Dedicated Host", "Use one larger VM instead of two", "Turn on Azure Backup"), setOf(0), "An availability set spreads VMs across fault domains (separate racks with their own power and network switch) and update domains (groups rebooted at different times), so one failure or update never stops both VMs. It carries a 99.95 percent SLA.",
                scenario = "Saltmarsh Shipping must keep its customs-filing app in a particular region for legal reasons, and that region has no availability zones.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "One dedicated host is one physical server; if it fails, both VMs stop.",
                    2 to "One VM is a single point of failure, however large.",
                    3 to "Backup helps you recover data later; it does not keep the app running.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: compute
            B.q("sa-mk34", "sa-compute", "Small pieces of code must run when files land in storage, scale out automatically, and cost next to nothing while idle. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Functions on the Flex Consumption plan", "A VM scale set", "Azure Kubernetes Service", "Azure Functions on the Premium plan"), setOf(0), "Azure Functions is serverless: you write a function, Azure runs it when a trigger fires, such as a new blob, and scales it out with demand. The Flex Consumption plan can scale to zero, so you pay little or nothing while nothing happens.",
                scenario = "Quillmark Publishing receives manuscripts as files a few times a day. Each must be checked and converted to PDF. Some days nothing arrives; launch days bring thousands.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Scale sets run whole VMs you pay for even while idle, and you manage the operating system.",
                    2 to "AKS is a full Kubernetes cluster to run and pay for, far too much for small functions.",
                    3 to "The Premium plan keeps at least one instance always warm, so you pay while idle.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk35", "sa-compute", "Several containerised microservices must scale on HTTP traffic and queue length, scale to zero, and the team does not want to manage Kubernetes. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Container Apps", "Azure Kubernetes Service", "Azure Container Instances", "Azure Batch"), setOf(0), "Container Apps runs containers on a managed platform built on Kubernetes, but hides the cluster. It scales on HTTP traffic or events such as queue length, can scale to zero, and supports Dapr and revisions out of the box.",
                scenario = "Kestrel Games' backend is six small services in containers. Traffic is near zero overnight and huge after a game update. The team of four has no Kubernetes experience.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "AKS gives the full Kubernetes control the team does not want to manage.",
                    2 to "Container Instances runs single container groups with no built-in autoscaling across services.",
                    3 to "Batch is for large parallel compute jobs, not always-on web services.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk36", "sa-compute", "Thousands of independent simulation tasks must run on hundreds of VMs, with the jobs queued and the pool of machines scaled automatically. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Batch", "Azure Functions", "Azure Logic Apps", "Azure App Service"), setOf(0), "Azure Batch is made for large-scale parallel and HPC (high-performance computing) work: it manages a pool of VMs, queues tasks onto them, and scales the pool up and down.",
                scenario = "Tidewell Energy runs 20,000 weather simulations each weekend to plan turbine maintenance. Each simulation takes about 40 minutes on one machine and is independent of the others.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Functions suit short event-driven code, not hundreds of machines running 40-minute tasks.",
                    2 to "Logic Apps orchestrates workflows between services; it is not a compute farm.",
                    3 to "App Service hosts web apps and APIs, not batch compute jobs.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk37", "sa-compute", "Overnight rendering jobs can be stopped and restarted at any time, and cost must be as low as possible. Which VM option do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Spot Virtual Machines", "Reserved VM instances for three years", "Azure Dedicated Host", "Standard pay-as-you-go VMs"), setOf(0), "Spot VMs use Azure's spare capacity at a deep discount. Azure can take them back at short notice, which is fine for work that can be interrupted and restarted.",
                scenario = "Larchmere Media renders video effects overnight. Each job saves its progress every few minutes, so a stopped job simply resumes later. The finance team wants the lowest possible price.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Reservations save money on machines that run all the time, a poor fit for a few hours a night.",
                    2 to "A dedicated host is a whole physical server for isolation needs, and it costs more.",
                    3 to "Pay-as-you-go works but costs far more than Spot for interruptible work.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: application architecture
            B.q("sa-mk38", "sa-app-architecture", "Orders must be processed in sequence per customer, never twice, and moved aside if they keep failing. Which messaging service do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Service Bus queues with sessions and duplicate detection", "Azure Queue Storage with a Functions trigger that moves failures to a poison queue", "Azure Event Grid with retry policies and dead-lettering to storage", "Azure Event Hubs with the customer ID as the partition key"), setOf(0), "Service Bus is an enterprise message broker. Sessions keep messages for the same customer in order, duplicate detection drops repeats, and messages that keep failing move to a dead-letter queue for a person to look at.",
                scenario = "Quayside Retail's checkout sends orders to a fulfilment service. Two orders from the same customer must be handled in the order they were placed, a payment must never be taken twice, and broken orders must not block the line.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "The poison queue moves failures aside, but Queue Storage has no ordering guarantee, sessions or duplicate detection.",
                    2 to "Event Grid can retry and dead-letter, but it gives no ordering guarantee and no duplicate detection.",
                    3 to "A partition key keeps one customer's events in order, but Event Hubs has no duplicate detection and no dead-letter queue.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk39", "sa-app-architecture", "Millions of telemetry events per second must be ingested, kept for a few days, and read by several independent consumers, some using Kafka clients. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Event Hubs", "Azure Service Bus topics", "Azure Event Grid", "Azure Queue Storage"), setOf(0), "Event Hubs is a big-data streaming service. It ingests millions of events per second, keeps them for a retention period, lets each consumer group read at its own pace, and speaks the Apache Kafka protocol.",
                scenario = "Glimmerton Games' clients send gameplay telemetry continuously. The analytics team, the anti-cheat team and a Kafka-based tool all need to read the same stream.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Service Bus suits business messages, not millions of telemetry events per second.",
                    2 to "Event Grid routes discrete events to handlers; it does not keep a replayable stream.",
                    3 to "Queue Storage hands each message to one reader and cannot handle this throughput.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk40", "sa-app-architecture", "Partner companies must call the internal APIs with their own subscription keys, a limit on calls per minute, and a portal to read the docs. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure API Management", "Azure Front Door", "Azure Application Gateway", "Azure Load Balancer"), setOf(0), "API Management is a gateway in front of your APIs. It issues subscription keys, enforces policies such as rate limits, and gives partners a developer portal with documentation and a test console.",
                scenario = "Driftmoor Logistics wants to let 50 retail partners book deliveries through its APIs. Each partner needs its own key, no partner may flood the system, and partners need self-service documentation.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Front Door accelerates and protects global web traffic, but has no subscription keys or developer portal.",
                    2 to "Application Gateway balances web traffic and adds a firewall, but does not manage API products or keys.",
                    3 to "Load Balancer spreads network traffic. It knows nothing about APIs or partners.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk41", "sa-app-architecture", "Product pages hit the database thousands of times a minute for data that changes once an hour. How do you cut database load and response times?", listOf("Add Azure Managed Redis as a cache in front of the database", "Scale the database up to a larger tier", "Add Azure Front Door in front of the database", "Move the product data to the archive tier"), setOf(0), "A cache keeps hot data in memory. Azure Managed Redis, which replaces the older Azure Cache for Redis tiers, answers repeat reads in about a millisecond, so most requests never reach the database.",
                scenario = "Quayside Retail's product catalogue is read constantly but updated hourly. During sales, database usage hits 100 percent and pages take seconds to load.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A bigger database costs much more and still repeats the same reads.",
                    2 to "Front Door caches web content at the edge; it does not sit in front of a database.",
                    3 to "Archive takes hours to read back, which is the opposite of what pages need.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: migrations
            B.q("sa-mk42", "sa-migrations", "Before migrating, you must discover 400 VMware VMs, size them for Azure, estimate monthly cost and see which servers talk to each other. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Migrate, with its appliance for discovery, assessment and dependency analysis", "Azure Site Recovery, with its replication appliance deployed in the VMware estate", "Azure Advisor, with its right-sizing and cost recommendations for each server", "Azure Data Box, with an offline copy of every VM disk shipped to Azure"), setOf(0), "Azure Migrate is the hub for migration. Its lightweight appliance discovers servers, assessments suggest Azure sizes and monthly costs, and dependency analysis shows which servers talk to each other, so you can move them together.",
                scenario = "Brackenridge Tools plans to leave its data centre next year. Nobody has an accurate list of its 400 VMware VMs, what they would cost to run in Azure, or which ones depend on which.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Site Recovery replicates machines for disaster recovery. It has no dependency analysis and is not the migration assessment tool.",
                    2 to "Advisor gives tips for resources already in Azure, not on-premises servers.",
                    3 to "Data Box ships data on a device; it does not discover or assess anything.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk43", "sa-migrations", "About 60 TB of archive files must reach Azure Blob Storage within three weeks, but the office internet link is slow and shared. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Data Box", "AzCopy over the existing internet link", "A site-to-site VPN and Robocopy", "Azure File Sync"), setOf(0), "Data Box is a secure, rugged device that Microsoft ships to you. You copy data onto it locally, send it back, and Microsoft loads it into your storage account. It is the answer when the network would take too long.",
                scenario = "Larchmere Media holds 60 TB of old footage on a NAS in an office with a 100 Mbps link that staff use all day. The building's lease ends in a month.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Even at full speed the link needs well over a month for 60 TB, and staff need it during the day.",
                    2 to "A VPN still runs over the same slow link.",
                    3 to "File Sync keeps shares in step over the network; it cannot beat the slow link either.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk44", "sa-migrations", "In which Cloud Adoption Framework phase do you build the landing zone: the subscriptions, network, identity and policies that workloads will move into?", listOf("Ready", "Strategy", "Plan", "Manage"), setOf(0), "In the Cloud Adoption Framework (CAF), the Ready phase prepares the environment: you build Azure landing zones, the ready-made foundation of subscriptions, networking, identity and governance, before workloads move in during Adopt.",
                scenario = "Copperleaf Foods has agreed why it is moving to Azure and which apps go first. Now the platform team must prepare subscriptions, hub networking and guardrails before the first app moves.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Strategy is about why you are moving and what the business expects, already done here.",
                    2 to "Plan is about which workloads move and the skills needed, also done here.",
                    3 to "Manage is about running workloads once they are in Azure.",
                ),
            ),
            // Infrastructure: networks
            B.q("sa-mk45", "sa-network-connectivity", "The connection to Azure must not cross the public internet, and must offer predictable latency and high bandwidth. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure ExpressRoute", "A site-to-site VPN", "A point-to-site VPN", "Public endpoints protected by a firewall"), setOf(0), "ExpressRoute is a private connection from your network to Microsoft through a connectivity provider. Traffic never travels over the public internet, so latency is steady and bandwidth can be very high.",
                scenario = "Harrowgate Bank's trading systems in its own data centre exchange large volumes of data with Azure. The risk team will not allow that traffic on the public internet, even encrypted.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "A site-to-site VPN is encrypted but still travels over the public internet.",
                    2 to "Point-to-site connects single computers, also over the internet.",
                    3 to "Public endpoints are reached over the internet by definition.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk46", "sa-network-connectivity", "Forty branch offices and three Azure regions must all connect to each other, with as little hand-built routing as possible. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Virtual WAN with a hub in each region", "A separate VPN gateway in each region and manual peering between every pair", "ExpressRoute to each branch office", "Azure Bastion in each region"), setOf(0), "Virtual WAN is a Microsoft-managed hub-and-spoke network. Branches, VNets and users connect to regional hubs, and the hubs route between each other automatically, giving any-to-any connectivity without hand-built routes.",
                scenario = "Driftmoor Logistics has 40 depots, each with a router that supports site-to-site VPN, and workloads in three Azure regions. Today every new depot takes the network team a week to wire up.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Gateways and pairwise peering everywhere is exactly the hand-built routing to avoid.",
                    2 to "ExpressRoute circuits for 40 small depots would be very expensive and slow to order.",
                    3 to "Bastion is for remote admin access to VMs, not site connectivity.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk47", "sa-network-connectivity", "Admins must open RDP and SSH sessions to VMs that have no public IP addresses, from a browser, without a VPN. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Bastion", "A public IP on each VM with a network security group rule", "A NAT gateway", "Azure Front Door"), setOf(0), "Azure Bastion is a managed jump service inside your VNet. Admins connect through the Azure portal over TLS, and Bastion opens RDP or SSH to the VM's private address, so no VM needs a public IP.",
                scenario = "Wrenfield Clinics' security policy bans public IP addresses on VMs. Its admins work from home and need occasional access to 30 servers.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Public IPs on VMs are exactly what the policy bans.",
                    2 to "A NAT gateway handles outbound traffic from VMs, not inbound admin sessions.",
                    3 to "Front Door is a global entry point for web apps, not for RDP or SSH.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk48", "sa-network-optimize", "A web app runs in two regions. Users worldwide must reach the closest healthy region, with caching at the edge and a web application firewall. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Front Door Premium", "Azure Application Gateway in each region", "Azure Traffic Manager", "Azure Load Balancer"), setOf(0), "Front Door is a global entry point at Microsoft's edge locations. It sends each user to the fastest healthy backend, fails over between regions, caches content close to users, and its Premium tier includes a web application firewall (WAF).",
                scenario = "Pebblecrest Hotels runs its booking site in West Europe and Australia East. Guests everywhere want fast pages, and the site must keep working if one region goes down. Bots keep attacking the login page.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Application Gateway is regional, so on its own it cannot route users between two regions.",
                    2 to "Traffic Manager steers by DNS only; it does not cache content or include a WAF.",
                    3 to "Load Balancer works at the network layer with no caching or WAF.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk49", "sa-network-optimize", "An app in a VNet, and analysts on-premises over ExpressRoute, must reach an Azure SQL database only through a private IP address, with public access turned off. What do you recommend?", listOf("A private endpoint for the database in the VNet, with private DNS", "A virtual network service endpoint for Microsoft.Sql", "A firewall rule allowing the office's public IP", "A NAT gateway on the app's subnet"), setOf(0), "A private endpoint gives the database a private IP address inside your VNet through Azure Private Link. Anything that can reach the VNet, including on-premises networks over ExpressRoute or VPN, can use it, and public access can be switched off.",
                scenario = "Thistledown Health's compliance team requires that patient databases have no public endpoint. Both the cloud app and analysts in the hospital must reach them.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "Service endpoints only help traffic from inside the VNet's subnets, and the database keeps a public address.",
                    2 to "A public IP rule still leaves the database on its public endpoint.",
                    3 to "A NAT gateway shapes outbound internet traffic; it does not make a database private.",
                ),
            ),
            B.q("sa-mk50", "sa-network-optimize", "All outbound internet traffic from spoke VNets must pass through one central point that only allows approved domain names and logs every connection. What do you recommend?", listOf("Azure Firewall in the hub VNet, with user-defined routes sending spoke traffic to it", "Network security groups on every spoke subnet, using service tags for the approved sites", "A NAT gateway in the hub VNet, with the spoke VNets peered to the hub", "Azure Firewall in the hub VNet, with the spoke VNets peered to it and using their default routes"), setOf(0), "Azure Firewall is a managed, stateful firewall. Placed in the hub, with route tables pointing spoke traffic at it, it can allow outbound traffic only to approved FQDNs (fully qualified domain names, such as api.example.com) and log every decision centrally.",
                scenario = "Corvelle Insurance has a hub-and-spoke network with 12 spoke VNets. The security team wants servers to reach only a short list of update and partner websites, and wants one log of all outbound connections.",
                whyWrong = mapOf(
                    1 to "NSGs filter by IP address, port and service tag, not by domain name, and are managed subnet by subnet with no central point.",
                    2 to "A NAT gateway serves subnets in its own VNet only, and it translates addresses without allowing or blocking sites by domain name.",
                    3 to "Peering connects the networks but does not steer spoke traffic to the firewall. That needs user-defined routes with the firewall as next hop.",
                ),
            ),
        ) + Az305Mock2.questions),
    )
}
